// TEST: ML Equiv Typed Partial Annotation
import ml as ml
// ============================================================================
// B3 OF plan-equivariance-in-types.md: THE FIRST RECALL WIN THE TYPED WALKER
// IS SUPPOSED TO HAVE, AND THE SEAM CANNOT.
//
// `twice_partial` has NO parameter annotation at all. What closes it is the
// ANNOTATED RETURN TYPE, forcing the parameter through the body: `x + x` has
// `x`'s type, the declared return unifies with it, and `x` is
// `Array<Float like IrrepsIdx<S>>` by the time the DECLARATION closes — which
// is the moment the typed deduction runs. No call site is consulted.
//
// The single parameter is load-bearing, not minimalism. Two things it rules
// out, and the file is only a test of the first:
//
//   * A CALL-SITE-closed parameter is a different and later problem. The
//     binder is still an open inference variable when the declaration closes,
//     so the deduction would have to wait for the monomorphized call — the
//     late tier of plan §2's early/late split, deferred this round.
//   * A parameter closed only through a SCALAR product does not close either.
//     Write this as `f(x, s: Float) -> Array<... IrrepsIdx<S>> = x * s` and
//     the rank does not propagate backwards from the annotated return across
//     the broadcast, so `x` lands as a scalar. The return annotation closes a
//     parameter when the body carries the parameter's own shape, and `x + x`
//     does.
//
// Stage 6a sees none of it, and not by oversight: `certSigOf` refuses ANY
// unannotated parameter outright, because the elaboration seam runs before
// unification and has nothing to classify. So the seam is silent here and this
// file carries no `// SUGGEST:` line — the whole of plan §0.1, "partial
// annotation dies as a limitation", in one function.
//
// The typed walker runs after unification, where `x` is a closed rep of spec S
// and `x + x` is the transfer table's same-spec addition arm, so `twice_partial`
// judges equivariant under O(3) and the typed channel proposes it.
//
// TYPED-SUGGEST is the differential's pin for exactly this: a proposal the
// typed side makes and the seam does not. It is strict in both directions, so
// this file also asserts that the typed walker proposes NOTHING ELSE here.
//
// PROPOSE ⊆ CHECK-ACCEPT, pinned the way corpus 053 pins it: `twice_tight` is
// the same body with the parameter annotated and the proposed conjunct written
// out, and it CHECKS — under the seam checker, which stays the checking
// authority through all of phase B. The typed proposal is therefore a
// hypothesis the shipped checker accepts, not a new theorem. The two EXPECTs
// are the same numbers, which is the other half: a proposal changes no value.
// ============================================================================
let static S = [(0, 0, 1), (1, 1, 1)]

// TYPED-SUGGEST: twice_partial|O3
function twice_partial(x) -> Array<Float like IrrepsIdx<S>> =
    x + x

// The pinned twin: the same map, annotated, carrying the conjunct the typed
// walker proposes. No SUGGEST line — a certified function is checked, not a
// candidate — and no TYPED-SUGGEST line either, for the same reason on the
// other side.
function twice_tight(x: Array<Float like IrrepsIdx<S>>)
                     where ml.equiv(O3) -> Array<Float like IrrepsIdx<S>> =
    x + x

let xv = [1.0, 4.0, 0.0 - 2.0, 6.0]

let p = twice_partial(xv)
// EXPECT: p = [2, 8, -4, 12]
let t = twice_tight(xv)
// EXPECT: t = [2, 8, -4, 12]
